### Controlling the Driver Gate with Arduino C++

Let's write a robust control sketch that commands an external driver switch connected to digital pin 9. The sketch demonstrates safe digital gating, periodic motor duty cycles, and real-time serial status logging.

```cpp
// Motor Driver Gate Control Sketch
// Digital Pin 9 commands the base/gate of an external driver switch
const int motorControlPin = 9;
const int runDurationMs = 2000;   // 2 seconds active run
const int restDurationMs = 1000;  // 1 second cooling pause

void setup() {
  // Initialize driver control line as a clean digital OUTPUT
  pinMode(motorControlPin, OUTPUT);
  // Ensure motor starts in a safe, de-energized state
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, LOW);

  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println(F("--- Motor Driver Gate Controller Initialized ---"));
  Serial.println(F("Pin 9 delivers 5V logic signal to external driver"));
}

void loop() {
  // Step 1: Engage driver switch (Motor energizes from external rail)
  Serial.println(F("STATUS: Driver gate HIGH -> Motor Spinning"));
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, HIGH);
  delay(runDurationMs);

  // Step 2: Disengage driver switch (Motor de-energizes safely)
  Serial.println(F("STATUS: Driver gate LOW -> Motor Stopped"));
  digitalWrite(motorControlPin, LOW);
  delay(restDurationMs);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `pinMode(motorControlPin, OUTPUT)`: Configures Pin 9 to drive high/low voltage logic levels.
- `digitalWrite(motorControlPin, LOW)` in `setup()`: Guarantees that upon Arduino power-up or reboot, the motor remains completely de-energized until logic execution begins.
- `digitalWrite(motorControlPin, HIGH)`: Emits a 5V logic HIGH signal that delivers ~2 mA to switch on the external driver transistor. The motor draws its operating current from the external supply rail, completely sparing the Arduino pin.
- `delay(restDurationMs)`: Enforces an off-period to prevent thermal buildup in the motor brushes and driver silicon.
